---
/**
 * website/src/components/ConsentBanner.astro — the consent banner (website plan, Task W1.12;
 * reconciliation findings 16, 18 and 55; decisions D93 and D106).
 *
 * D93 chose analytics for the site and answered the question that comes with it: nothing is
 * requested from the analytics origin until the visitor consents. The gate itself is code,
 * and since W3.2 it lives in the site's one first-party script file — `public/scripts/site.js`,
 * because D183 admits no inline script and the deployed policy needs no hash for one. This
 * component is the markup that code drives: it carries the consent key, the property id and
 * the one admitted origin as data attributes, it is the only thing that writes a decision,
 * and it is rendered only when `site.yaml` says analytics are on — so with the flag off the
 * artifact carries no banner, no configuration and no analytics origin at all.
 *
 * The decisions this file makes, and why:
 *
 *   - **Two choices, equally offered.** Both are native `<button type="button">` elements,
 *     and the consent rules make them a pair of equals: one shared rule gives them the same
 *     box — the same height, the same padding, the same type, one column each of the pair's
 *     grid, both always drawn — and only the fill and the label's colour tell the primary
 *     ("Accept all") from the secondary ("Decline"). There is no "reject all" buried in a
 *     settings page and no pre-ticked box: accepting and declining are one press each, and
 *     neither is the one the layout nudges a visitor towards by being easier to reach.
 *   - **Dismissal is a refusal.** Escape hides the banner and records `denied`, and a
 *     visitor who navigates away without choosing loads nothing either — the default when
 *     nobody chooses is that nothing is measured, because the gate needs a stored `granted`
 *     to fetch anything at all.
 *   - **Nothing is loaded to ask the question.** The markup carries no script and names no
 *     host that is fetched; the gate that wakes on a choice is `site.js`, and it is gated in
 *     turn. The two attributes below are the whole of the gate's configuration, and
 *     `website/test/origins.mjs` holds the origin to the one the deployed policy admits.
 *   - **Storage that is refused is treated as no decision.** Reads and writes are wrapped,
 *     the write is read back (a private-mode browser may accept a write and keep nothing),
 *     and when the choice cannot be kept the banner stays, says so in a `role="status"`
 *     line, and loads nothing. The visitor is asked again rather than answered for.
 *   - **No focus trap, and no focus stolen.** The banner does not take focus on load, does
 *     not intercept Tab, and on a choice it moves focus to `<main>` — the content the
 *     visitor asked for — rather than leaving it on a control that has just vanished.
 *   - **Withdrawal.** On the privacy page the banner is offered even when a decision is
 *     stored (`alwaysOffer`), which is how consent is withdrawn: the one page that says how
 *     is the one page where the choice is always available again. It carries the campaign
 *     decision's key as well as the site's (`marketingKey`), so the refusal it offers takes
 *     back both answers — a campaign decision is made on a campaign page, where the bar is
 *     gone once it has been answered, and this is the page a visitor comes to to undo one.
 *   - **One question, in two sentences.** The bar asks about measurement as a whole — cookies
 *     and identifiers, Google Analytics 4, and the marketing trackers and the booking widget
 *     the campaign pages carry — in the same two sentences on every page, rather than in four
 *     questions a reader has to go looking for. Nothing in it promises that a vendor is
 *     running: the ids come from `site.yaml` and both are the owner's now (supplied 2026-10-31),
 *     so every vendor the bar names is one the site is configured to load — the owner's
 *     feedback of 2026-10-30 — while a loader with no id fetches nothing and a tracker with no
 *     id is not named. What a bar's own refusal covers is the statement's to say,
 *     not the bar's: on the privacy page, where the campaign key is carried without the
 *     campaign configuration, declining takes the campaign answer back and never gives one.
 *
 * The markup is emitted hidden and `site.js` reveals it: a browser that runs no script shows
 * no control it cannot honour, and loads nothing.
 */
interface Props {
  /** Offer the choice even when a decision is stored: the privacy page's job (withdrawal). */
  alwaysOffer?: boolean
  /** The analytics property the gate reports to, from `site.yaml` (W3.2). */
  measurementID: string
  /** The one third-party origin the gate may load from (D93, D106). */
  analyticsOrigin: string
  /**
   * The campaign measurement's configuration, on the four campaign pages only (G2.3, D401):
   * its own consent key, the page it was built for, the two vendor ids and the three origins
   * their loaders fetch from, and the booking link the embed is built from. Absent everywhere
   * else, which is why every other page is unchanged by that task.
   */
  marketing?: MarketingConfig | null
  /**
   * The campaign decision's key where the bar does not ask the campaign question — the privacy
   * page, which is where a visitor withdraws one. A bar carrying the key without the
   * configuration answers that question in the refusal direction only: `site.js` may take the
   * campaign decision away from it, and may not give one, because this page never put the three
   * vendors to the visitor. Null on every other page.
   */
  marketingKey?: string | null
}

export interface MarketingConfig {
  /** The key the campaign decision is stored under (`consent-key.mjs`). */
  key: string
  /** The route this configuration was built for: the loader runs on that page and no other. */
  path: string
  metaID: string
  metaOrigin: string
  linkedinID: string
  linkedinOrigin: string
  /** `landing.yaml`'s booking link, which the inline embed is built from. */
  calendlyURL: string
  /** The origin Calendly's widget script and stylesheet come from. */
  calendlyOrigin: string
}

const {
  alwaysOffer = false,
  measurementID,
  analyticsOrigin,
  marketing = null,
  marketingKey = null,
} = Astro.props

// The key the decision is stored under, written once in `src/lib/consent-key.mjs`: `site.js`
// reads it back out of the markup below, so the banner that writes the decision and the gate
// that reads it cannot drift apart.
import { CONSENT_KEY } from '../lib/consent-key.mjs'
---

<aside
  class="consent-banner"
  id="consent-banner"
  role="region"
  aria-label="Measurement consent"
  data-consent-key={CONSENT_KEY}
  data-consent-always={alwaysOffer ? 'true' : undefined}
  data-analytics-id={measurementID}
  data-analytics-origin={analyticsOrigin}
  data-marketing-consent-key={marketing?.key ?? marketingKey ?? undefined}
  data-marketing-path={marketing?.path}
  data-meta-pixel-id={marketing?.metaID}
  data-meta-origin={marketing?.metaOrigin}
  data-linkedin-partner-id={marketing?.linkedinID}
  data-linkedin-origin={marketing?.linkedinOrigin}
  data-calendly-url={marketing?.calendlyURL}
  data-calendly-origin={marketing?.calendlyOrigin}
  hidden
>
  <div class="consent-banner__body">
    <div class="consent-banner__inner">
      <p class="consent-banner__text">
        This site measures visits with cookies and identifiers: <strong>Google Analytics</strong> on every page, and marketing trackers, <strong>Meta</strong>'s pixel, <strong>LinkedIn</strong>'s insight tag and <strong>Calendly</strong>'s booking widget, on the four campaign pages. Nothing loads until you accept. <a href="/privacy/">What this site measures</a>
      </p>
      <div class="consent-banner__choices">
        <button type="button" class="consent-banner__button consent-banner__button--accept" data-consent="granted">
          Accept all
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="consent-banner__button consent-banner__button--decline" data-consent="denied">
          Decline
        </button>
      </div>
      <p class="consent-banner__status" id="consent-banner-status" role="status" hidden>
        Your browser is not storing this choice, so this site cannot keep it: nothing is loaded, and you will be asked again on your next visit.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</aside>

<style>
  /* The banner is a compact bar in the page's own flow, at the very top, above the header (the
     owner's ruling of 2026-10-23, the redesign's Step 3: it must not cover the first screen's
     content). It used to be fixed over the foot of the window, where it covered whatever was
     there at every scroll position.

     `.consent-banner[hidden]` keeps it out of the page whatever `display` the bar draws with (its
     grid is the collapse below); the one rule that draws it is the early decision's mark on `<html>`
     (`public/scripts/consent-early.js`), set before the page is drawn, so the bar is in its
     place from the first frame and nothing below it moves when it appears. `site.js` removes the
     mark and sets `hidden` when the visitor decides. With scripts off the mark is never set and
     the bar is never drawn, and nothing can load from Google either.

     It is a tone step above the ground (the panel), with no rule under it. */
  .consent-banner {
    /* In the flow, and still on the chrome's layer (tokens.css): the bar is the reader's own
       chrome, drawn above the header's panel when a group opens under it. */
    position: relative;
    z-index: var(--z-chrome);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: 1fr;
    padding-inline: var(--frame-gutter);
    background: var(--panel);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* A choice collapses the bar instead of snapping it away (owner-approved motion opportunities
     2026-10-23): `site.js` marks it closing and sets `hidden` once the collapse has run. The row
     goes from its height to none while the bar fades, so the page below rises with it. The bar's
     vertical padding is the inner box's, inside the clip, so the row can reach zero. */
  .consent-banner__body {
    min-height: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .consent-banner[data-consent-closing] {
    grid-template-rows: 0fr;
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* For a reader who asked for less motion the bar only fades, and the layout's reduce rule
     makes that fade 1ms; the row collapses only where motion is welcome. */
  .consent-banner {
    transition: opacity var(--duration-collapse) var(--ease-out);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .consent-banner {
      transition:
        grid-template-rows var(--duration-collapse) var(--ease-out),
        opacity var(--duration-collapse) var(--ease-out);
    }
  }

  .consent-banner[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  :global(html[data-consent-ask]) .consent-banner[hidden] {
    display: grid;
  }

  .consent-banner__inner {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-md);
    padding-block: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .consent-banner__text {
    flex: 1 1 28rem;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  /* The pair is a two-column grid, so the two choices are the same size whatever their labels
     say — a consent rule rather than a preference — and it grows into the whole row when the
     bar wraps at phone width, where each choice is half of it. `flex: 1 1 14rem` is the
     basis that wraps: beside the text there is room for a 14 rem pair, and on a phone the pair
     takes the line of its own that the wrap gives it. */
  .consent-banner__choices {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    flex: 1 1 14rem;
    max-width: 22rem;
  }

  /* The two choices are one control drawn twice: this rule is the whole box — the height the
     owner's brief measures every control against, the padding, the type, no outline — and the
     two fills below change nothing about it, so neither choice is easier to press than the
     other however the pair is drawn. */
  .consent-banner__button {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: var(--space-3xs) var(--space-sm);
    border: 0;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    white-space: nowrap;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out), transform var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }

  /* The primary: the accent as the fill and the ground as the label, the pair the site's own
     buttons use (`Hero.astro`'s `.hero-cta`, and `base.css`'s skip link). */
  .consent-banner__button--accept {
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--ground);
  }

  /* The secondary: the shape the bar's choices were, a tone step above the bar. It is the same
     box as the primary and a step quieter, which is the whole of the difference between them. */
  .consent-banner__button--decline {
    background: var(--surface);
    color: var(--accent-on-dark);
  }

  .consent-banner__button:active {
    transform: scale(0.97);
  }

  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .consent-banner__button--accept:hover {
      background: var(--accent-on-dark);
    }

    .consent-banner__button--decline:hover {
      background: var(--border);
      color: var(--text);
    }
  }

  .consent-banner__status {
    flex-basis: 100%;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--warn);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
  }
</style>
